From c417784d68df513822c18c2a45dbd03f57d37542 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nuk3s <44327449+nuk3s@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 22:08:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] dsv41: structured output (json_object, json_schema with
 numeric bounds, guided_choice) with drafts on

The engine answered every structured request with HTTP 400 (issue #22): no Zig family set Info.structures, so
server.zig refused response_format, guided_json, guided_choice and structured_outputs before any token. A client that
sends a json_schema with minimum and maximum on a number got the same 400.

Rank 0 now keeps a DeepSeek-V4.1 reply inside its request's grammar, with DSpark drafts on, for response_format
json_object and json_schema, guided_json, structured_outputs json and choice, guided_choice and Anthropic
output_config.format. regex and grammar (EBNF) stay HTTP 400. There is no switch: a request without a grammar runs as
before (its walk is acceptPath itself, and the masked samplers run only for structured streams).

- core/grammar compiles the grammar to a byte matcher. json_object is xgrammar 0.2.8's root object. json_schema
  follows xgrammar 0.2.8's strict rules (max_whitespace_cnt 32), with minimum, maximum, exclusiveMinimum and
  exclusiveMaximum on integer and number as xgrammar's range regex takes them (bounds.zig). guided_choice is a byte
  trie. A table gives each token's bytes (1.48 MB on rank 0, built once at start). Added tokens (</think>, the DSML
  markers) are never allowed.
- The server compiles the program into the request's arena. A keyword the engine cannot enforce gets HTTP 400 with
  its JSON location, before any header. docs/api.md lists what is enforced and what is refused.
- After each verify the lane core walks the accepted path under the grammar. A greedy row keeps its draw when the
  grammar allows it; every other row is drawn again among the allowed tokens (new optional Backend.VTable.redraw).
  The first token and the token after a forced window get the same mask. dsv41 draws again on rank 0 from logits it
  already holds. Rank 1 never sees the grammar.
- The masked draw equals the plain draw on a copy of the row with the refused tokens at -inf, and a refused draft
  never equals the masked pick. So a reply is the same drafted, with "draft": false and in a shared round.
- A token the grammar refuses ends only its own stream (HTTP 500 "the reply's grammar refused its next token", or an
  error event when streaming). Under a grammar the thinking budget's close stops at </think>. ignore_eos with a
  grammar gets HTTP 400, because the end token must close the reply.
- With 0004: a grammar beside tool_choice "required" or a named function gets HTTP 400, so the call gate never meets
  a grammar.
- dsv41's prefillPart clears the redraw rows at a pass's start and sets the prompt row after the first draw, so a
  pass in pieces (TF_FILL_CHUNKS) and a cut-in leave only the prompt row for a redraw, as a whole pass does.

Tests (x86_64, no GPU): two frozen corpora that xgrammar 0.2.8 labelled (3,000 json_object strings; 10,350 schema,
choice and bounds strings) give xgrammar's verdict on every string, with the index of the refused byte;
tools/zig/xgrammar_json_corpus.py and xgrammar_schema_corpus.py write them, and --check compares them with an
installed xgrammar. On the fake target a json_object, json_schema or choice reply is the same drafted, with
"draft": false and in a shared round, greedy and sampled. A schema reply that decodes between the pieces of another
prompt's pass equals its reply alone, with TF_FILL_CHUNKS and with 0005's interleave.

The same change is open on bertholomus/TensorFold as #48, #49 and #50 (the bounds are #50's last commit). Drop this
patch when TF_REF has them.
